      "stop_reason": "end_turn",
      "tool_uses": [],
      "assistant_text": "Perfect! I found the answer. Based on the wiki article [[credential-scoping]]:\n\nThe Hub keeps the Anthropic API key out of its own process environment by **never holding it in the Hub process at all**. Instead:\n\n1. **A local secrets loader** reads the API key from a git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file at startup\n2. **The key is injected only into child processes** \u2014 specifically, into the environment of the spawned Ingest agent (and Query agent) processes, immediately before those processes start\n3. **The Hub\u0027s process environment is never mutated** \u2014 the key never lives in the Hub\u0027s own environment variables\n\nThis design provides a security boundary: even if the Hub process is compromised or misbehaves, an attacker cannot leak the credential by simply inspecting the Hub\u0027s environment variables, since the key only exists inside the short-lived child processes that actually need to call the Anthropic API.",
